# Unidirectional `@ManyToOne` in Spring Boot JPA

## Overview

In Spring Boot applications using JPA/Hibernate, relationships between entities can be defined as **unidirectional** or **bidirectional**.

This document explains what happens when:
- A `Post` entity defines a `@ManyToOne` relationship with `User`
- The `User` entity does **not** define a corresponding `@OneToMany` relationship

---

## Entity Design

### Post Entity (Owning Side)

```java
@Entity
public class Post {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id")
    private User user;
}
User Entity (No Collection Mapping)
@Entity
public class User {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    private String username;
}
What Happens Internally?
1. Application Behavior

✅ The application works correctly
✅ Entities are persisted without issues
✅ Hibernate manages the relationship properly

This works because @ManyToOne is the owning side of the relationship.

2. Database Schema

Hibernate generates:

A posts table

A user_id foreign key column in the posts table

A foreign key constraint referencing the users table

📌 No extra table is created.

3. Relationship Type

This is a Unidirectional Many-to-One relationship:

Navigation is possible from Post → User

Navigation is not possible from User → Post

4. Limitations Without @OneToMany

You cannot do the following:

user.getPosts(); // ❌ Not available

To fetch posts of a user, you must query explicitly:

List<Post> posts = postRepository.findByUser(user);

or

List<Post> posts = postRepository.findByUserId(userId);
Performance Considerations

✅ Often preferred in real-world applications
✅ Avoids unnecessary collection loading
✅ Reduces risk of N+1 query issues
✅ Keeps entities lightweight and focused

Many production systems intentionally avoid bidirectional mappings unless required.

When to Add @OneToMany

Add a bidirectional mapping only if:

You need navigation from User → Posts

You understand mappedBy, cascading, and fetch strategies

Example:

@OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
private List<Post> posts;

⚠ This does NOT create a new database column.

Best Practices

✔ Prefer unidirectional relationships by default
✔ Add bidirectional mappings only for real use cases
❌ Avoid adding @OneToMany just for symmetry

JPA relationships should model business access patterns, not just database structure.